title: Config ladders disagree on precedence; one bad key discards whole file
updated_at: "2026-08-23T15:53:23Z"
---
Found while coordinating the GH #55/56/58/59/60 batch, 2026-08-24. Verified by
reading src/config.rs on master @ 95d1d04. Pre-existing; not a regression.

## Two config ladders, opposite precedence

1. `UserPrefs::load` (src/config.rs:172-178) — user global FIRST, then
   workspace: **workspace wins**. Carries `default_mode`, `warnings_as_errors`.
   Its own doc comment still contains the unresolved note
   "(later wins? workspace should win)".
2. `LspConfig::load_with_parent` (src/config.rs:349-360) — parent config, then
   per-root config, then `load_user_config_file()` LAST: **user global wins**.
   Carries `openplanet_dir`, `plugins_dir`, `game_target`, `defines`,
   `source_paths`, `ignore_paths`, `shortcut_*`, `define_matrix`, ...

So a user-global `~/.config/openplanet-lsp/config.toml` with `source_paths` or
`defines` silently overrides a plugin's own `.openplanet-lsp.toml`. Every other
language tool (and the README's framing) puts project config above user config.
The layer-2b comment even documents the ladder as "parent config < per-root
config < user config", so the inversion is deliberate in code but almost
certainly not intended as UX.

## Impact
- A machine-wide `source_paths = ["src"]` breaks every plugin whose sources live
  elsewhere, with no local override possible.
- The two ladders disagree, so `warnings_as_errors` and `source_paths` obey
  opposite rules in the same file — undiscoverable without reading the source.
- New keys are landing around this: `unused_callbacks` (B012/GH #56) and
  `honor_gitignore` (B015/GH #60) both had to bypass the LspConfig ladder with a
  dedicated resolver to get workspace-wins. That workaround is now duplicated.

## Second, separate bug in the same area
`load_config_file` / `load_user_config_file` / `UserPrefs::apply_file` all do
`let Ok(cfg) = toml::from_str::<ConfigFile>(&contents) else { return }`. ONE
mistyped key (e.g. `source_paths = "src"` instead of `["src"]`) silently discards
the ENTIRE config file — every other key included — with no diagnostic. Users get
"my config does nothing" with no way to tell why.

## Work
- Decide the intended precedence (recommend: init options > CLI flag >
  per-root workspace > parent > user global > auto-detect) and make BOTH ladders
  obey it. Fold the per-key resolvers added for `unused_callbacks` /
  `honor_gitignore` back onto the shared path once it is correct.
- Report unparseable/mistyped config: keep the lenient per-key read so one bad
  key cannot nuke the file, and surface a diagnostic/warning naming the file, the
  key, and the expected type.
- Document the final ladder in README next to the config block.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: a workspace `.openplanet-lsp.toml` setting `source_paths = ["a"]` plus a
user-global setting `source_paths = ["b"]` must resolve to `["a"]` (fails today);
same for `defines`, `game_target`, `ignore_paths`. Variants: parent vs per-root in
a multi-root workspace; init options still highest; CLI flag still beats config;
one mistyped key leaves sibling keys in the SAME file effective (fails today);
malformed TOML still degrades to defaults without panicking. Precedence tests
must not mutate `$HOME` in-process (src/config.rs:1027 documents that as racy) —
use pure resolver helpers.